t tense of the  verb to be we get the pluperfect or past  perfect tense horrid Abood and they had  eaten there are other compound tenses as  well but the ones we've covered so far  should give you a general idea of how  the verb tenses work questions now if  you want to turn that sentence into a  question what  you do well you can simply use rising  intonation [ __ ] Abood and in literary  Persian you can add the yes/no question  marker oh yeah at the beginning of the  sentence are your dividend but what  about open-ended questions here are the  basic question words T who chair  what koja where K when Shara why cheat  or how padam which the question word  usually comes after the subject if the  subject is included in the sentence to  Jimmy Chloe what are you eating  word for word it you want to eat or in  the colloquial present progressive form  Tochigi me Chloe  how are you Harley Shamar chat or asked  here  Holly shoma is the subject which means  your condition then chat or meaning how  comes after the subject and the  third-person singular present tense of  the verb meaning to be comes at the end  so word for word it your condition how  is one important thing to notice in this  sentence here is that this phrase shows  another use of Azoff a using the  particle F to connect to words when we  saw it before it connected a noun and an  adjective but this time it connects a  noun to a pronoun to show possession is  off a can also be used to connect two  nouns with the second one adding meaning  to the first one it's other that's  meaning textbook the first word Kitab  means book as you know and the second  dass means lesson or course literally  you could translate it as lesson book or  course book now that we've examined some  of the key features of persian let's  look at one more sentence and see what  we find  this sentence means the children like  bread but they don't like vegetables but  Jehan and reduced or and very sad the  dirt reduce nagar and word for word its  children bred accusative marker they  like but vegetables accusative marker  they don't like first we have the word  for children but chi ha the singular is  bad cheer and as we saw before you add  ha to form the plural  next is the word for bread nun or noon  in informal speech and it's followed by  the accusative marker at the end of this  clause we have the verb as usual but  this verb is a little different than the  ones we saw before this is a compound  verb which is made up of two words  deuced  a noun meaning friend and - Dan the verb  meaning to have here conjugated for the  third person plural yes the verb for  like in Persian literally means have  friend if you examine its parts many  many verbs in Persian are compound verbs  like this which are made up of a verb  and another element like a noun or  adjective a few more examples sad is  that an head plus hit means to pay a  visit for sure then leg plus become  means to stand up then the e car then  life plus do means to live the most  common verb used to form compound verbs  is cat then compound verbs account for  most of the verbs used in Persian only  around a hundred simple verbs are used  on a regular basis and the rest are  compound verbs now back to the sentence  here we see the conjunction valley  meaning but next we see the plural noun  meaning vegetables sabzi chart the  singular form is sabzi so the regular  plural form is sabzi ha but when  referring to vegetables in general the  common word is sabzi chart which means  vegetables and herbs then next we see  the accusative marker again and then we  see the negative form of the compound  verb we saw a moment ago news Nagaland  to make a verb negative you just add nan  before the conjugated verb as you can  see Persian is quite a straightforward  language which might come as a surprise  to some of you who were initially scared  off by the script aside from the verbs  everything seems to follow regular  patterns without many exceptions and  even the verbs while there are some  complex and irregular forms work quite  logically this makes Persian seem like  quite a fun language to learn one of the  challenges is probably the difference  between the spoken colloquial language  and the written formal language because  the written formal language hasn't  really changed in hundreds of years but  it seems that most learners of Persian  get used to these differences without  too much trouble  the question of the day for native  speakers and fluent second language  speakers of Persian what other features  of Persian do you think are important or  valuable for us to know about and for  learners of Persian what's been your  experience with studying Persian what  have you found challenging and what have  you found enjoyable be sure to follow  Lange focus on Facebook Twitter and  Instagram and once again thank you to my  wonderful patreon supporters especially  these ones right here on the screen who  are my top tier patreon supporters many  special thanks to them and to everyone  thank you for watching and have a nice  day  [Music]
 

